---
import OsLayout from "../layouts/OsLayout.astro";
import PageHead from "../components/PageHead.astro";
import { ROUTES } from "../lib/site";
---

<OsLayout
  title="Not found"
  description="That path doesn't exist on this site."
  path="/404"
  noindex
>
  <PageHead eyebrow="Error 404" heading="No such path" />

  <div class="stack">
    {/*
      One span per line, and they have to stay written end to end with no
      whitespace between them. Inside a pre, the newline separating two tags is
      a real text node, so putting these on separate source lines would render
      a blank line between each of them once they are display: block. Do not
      let a formatter break this apart.
    */}
    <pre class="dump" aria-label="Terminal error output"><code><span class="dump__line">&gt; open <span class="dump__path" id="bad-path">the requested path</span></span><span class="dump__line">  status : 404</span><span class="dump__line">  reason : no route matches</span><span class="dump__line">  action : pick one of the four below</span></code></pre>

    <nav aria-label="Available pages">
      <ul class="routes">
        {
          ROUTES.map((route) => (
            <li>
              <a href={route.path}>{route.title}</a>
              <span class="routes__path">{route.path}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <p class="muted">
      If you followed a link here from somewhere else on this site, that is a
      bug and I would like to know about it.
    </p>
  </div>
</OsLayout>

<script>
  // Echo back what was actually requested. Written as text, never as HTML,
  // so a crafted URL cannot inject anything into the page.
  const slot = document.getElementById("bad-path");
  if (slot) slot.textContent = window.location.pathname;
</script>

<style>
  /*
    The dump wraps rather than scrolling sideways, so it stops at the same edge
    every other block on this page stops at.

    It used to be plain `pre` with `overflow-x: auto`, and at 320px the longest
    line ran 288.6px inside a 283px content box, so "below" was cut in half at
    the border while the heading, the route list and the note beside it all sat
    inside 286px. The scroll container was hiding it rather than solving it: a
    reader at that width saw a truncated word, not a scrollbar to drag.

    The first line is not fixed copy, which is what rules out simply sizing the
    box to fit. It echoes location.pathname, so its length is whatever was
    typed, and a long enough path overflows any width chosen here. pre-wrap
    plus overflow-wrap keeps that case inside the box too, and a terminal
    wrapping a line it cannot fit is the more faithful behaviour anyway.

    overflow-x is gone with it. With wrapping on, nothing can overflow, so the
    property could no longer fire and a rule that cannot fire is worse than no
    rule: it reads as protection that is not there.
  */
  .dump {
    border: 1px solid var(--amber-faint);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--red);
    background: var(--bg-inset);
    padding: var(--s4);
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    color: var(--amber-dim);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .dump code {
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    padding: 0;
    color: inherit;
    font-size: inherit;
  }

  /*
    A hanging indent, so a line too long for the box carries on underneath its
    own value instead of restarting at the left edge. Without it "below" landed
    in column 0 at 320px and read as a fifth line of output rather than as the
    rest of the fourth.

    11ch is the width of the label column: "  action : " and "  status : " are
    both exactly 11 characters, so a wrapped line resumes directly under where
    the value started. The same 11ch already sets the route column below, in a
    monospace block where a character is the unit that means something.

    Only the first line of a block takes text-indent, which is why each line is
    its own block above rather than one run of preformatted text.
  */
  .dump__line {
    display: block;
    padding-left: 11ch;
    text-indent: -11ch;
  }

  .dump__path {
    color: var(--red);
  }

  .routes {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s2);
  }

  .routes li {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--s3);
  }

  /*
    The paths form a column instead of trailing each name at whatever width it
    happens to be. Four rows of the same shape starting at four different x
    positions, a 54px spread at 1280, read as four unrelated lines rather than
    a table of the routes.

    11ch, in the row's own 15px body type, so it holds the longest name
    ("Experience", 10 characters) with a space to spare and rescales with the
    type rather than pinning to a magic pixel width. It is a floor, not a fixed
    width: nothing here can outgrow it, but a longer route name would push its
    own row wider rather than being clipped.
  */
  .routes li > a {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    min-width: 11ch;
  }

  .routes__path {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    color: var(--amber-dim);
  }
</style>
